add(61,'arukh-laner-additional-priestly-mitzvot',6,'Arukh LaNer on Yevamot 84b',
'דשאני כהנים דרבי בהן הכתוב מצות יתירות כגון עבודות ואכילת ק"ק דליכא בנשים וה"א גם לאו שאינה בכל דביאת איסור ליכא גבן',
'Priests are different, because Scripture imposed additional mitzvot upon them, such as the Temple services and eating offerings of the highest sanctity, which do not apply to women. One might therefore have thought that a prohibition not applying to everyone, concerning a forbidden union, would likewise not apply to them [the women].',
'In explaining the Gemara’s legal argument, Arukh LaNer identifies concrete examples of the priests’ additional mitzvot. The last clause is a possibility raised in that argument, not its final ruling.',P61,url='https://wiki.jewishbooks.org.il/mediawiki/wiki/ערוך_לנר/יבמות/פד/ב')
E[-1]['sourceEvidence'][0]['webRef']='turn54view0'
add(61,'tzidkat-priestly-holiness-and-extra-remedies',6,'Tzidkat HaTzadik 219:1',sub(he('Tzidkat HaTzadik 219:1'),'וכהנים רבה בהן','ומצות המלך'),
'Scripture imposed additional mitzvot upon the priests because Aharon was set apart to consecrate him as most holy. Correspondingly, his inclination is also distinct from the inclinations of other people’s thoughts, and he needs additional counsels to guard against it.',
'Rav Tzadok explains elsewhere why greater holiness is accompanied by additional obligations: the stronger spiritual capacity has its corresponding challenge and needs further remedies.',P61,url='#/read/Tzidkat_HaTzadik_219',readerRef='Tzidkat HaTzadik 219:1')
E[-1]['sourceEvidence'][0]['url']='https://www.sefaria.org/Tzidkat_HaTzadik_219:1'
add(61,'kad-hakemach-sanctification-through-commandments',8,'Kad HaKemach, Fringes 1:1',sub(he('Kad HaKemach, Fringes 1',1),'דבר ידוע','ומכלל המצות המקובלות'),
'It is well known that Israel’s holiness depends on the mitzvot. This is what the Sifrei says: “You shall sanctify yourselves”—this is the holiness of a mitzvah. The mitzvot through which we were commanded to sanctify ourselves are the commandments received through revelation, for these are the essence of holiness. It is concerning them that our Sages, the sages of truth, instituted the blessing, “Who sanctified us with His mitzvot and commanded us.” Because of these revealed commandments, Israel were called a holy nation at Sinai, as it is stated, “You shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation.” A people is not called a holy nation through fulfilling the rational commandments, for even the idolaters, who are “a nation bereft of counsel, with no understanding,” occupy themselves with some of the rational commandments. Nor did the Sages institute a blessing over the rational commandments, for the essential blessing and holiness belong to the revealed commandments.',
'Rabbenu Bachya distinguishes commandments known through revelation from duties accessible to reason. His explanation connects the blessing “Who sanctified us with His mitzvot” directly with the holiness produced by observance.',P61)
add(62,'resisei-inherited-spiritual-root',0,'Resisei Layla 47:1',local('resisei-layla','Resisei Layla 47:1',38,44),
'Avraham our father’s faith was manifest in his heart through the intensity of his own effort; that is why he is called the first of the converts. Nevertheless, our Sages said at the beginning of Bereshit Rabbah, Toldot, that Avraham was called Israel: he merited becoming like a native-born Israelite, whose quality is present from birth. This was after the birth of Yitzchak, through whom “your offspring shall be called,” and who was circumcised at eight days. That is, he was good from birth and circumcised for the blessed God before he possessed any understanding at all; this came from Avraham’s power within him. It therefore became clear that, through his own effort, Avraham our father had changed at his root to be this way, as though he were so from birth. For offspring resemble what their parent is in essence and at the root.',
'This explains how a quality acquired through a forefather’s effort can become an inherited spiritual root. The surrounding passage distinguishes that inherited faith from its full expression in the heart, which still requires effort.',P62,url='#/read/Resisei_Layla_47',readerRef='Resisei Layla 47:1')
E[-1]['sourceEvidence'][0]['contextHe']=local('resisei-layla','Resisei Layla 47:1',35,44)
add(62,'likkutei-joseph-and-amalek-persistence',7,'Likkutei Maamarim, Compositions on the Festivals 16:30',local('likkutei-maamarim','Likkutei Maamarim, Compositions on the Festivals 16:30',106,116),
'But Israel’s obstinacy proceeds from wisdom, through knowledge that connects wisdom with what is below it and with the powers of action. A person is obstinate only for the honor of Heaven; on the contrary, this becomes the channel through which precious souls emerge from Israel. Therefore “Amalek falls only into the hands of Yosef’s descendants.” Amalek is the root of obstinacy, and the main object of his accusation is Israel’s desires, as the Sages said on the verse “who encountered you.” But his obstinacy opposes the will of the blessed God, while the obstinacy of Yosef’s descendants accords with the will of the blessed God.',
'This parallel explains the reference “as I wrote elsewhere.” The decisive contrast is the purpose of persistence: Yosef’s line turns it toward the divine will, while Amalek turns it against that will.',P62,url='#/read/Likkutei_Maamarim,_Compositions_on_the_Festivals_16',readerRef='Likkutei Maamarim, Compositions on the Festivals 16:30')
add(62,'dover-davids-name-hasid',10,'Dover Tzedek, Kuntres Ner HaMitzvot 2:111',local('dover-tzedek','Dover Tzedek, Kuntres Ner HaMitzvot 2:111',0,6),
'Rather, it is in the manner said of Avraham our father: His two kidneys were like two springs, welling forth Torah. This comes only from being exceptionally pious, to the point that one does not need wisdom to awaken fear, as Rambam wrote at the beginning of chapter 2 of Hilkhot Yesodei HaTorah. Rather, he has reached the level at which “their hearts are in their control”: his heart is empty within him—the level of David the king, peace be upon him. For this reason he called himself “pious.” This is the definition of piety: that his heart is empty because he has slain the evil inclination, and he no longer desires any worldly desire, only what accords with the will of the blessed God. As it is written in the Zohar: “Who is pious? One who acts devotedly toward his Maker.”',
'Rav Tzadok explicitly connects David’s title hasid with a heart no longer ruled by self-directed desire. This gives the meaning of the title whose inheritance is discussed in the ois.',P62,url='#/read/Dover_Tzedek,_Kuntres_Ner_HaMitzvot_2',readerRef='Dover Tzedek, Kuntres Ner HaMitzvot 2:111')
zoharHoly='ובגין כך - "והיה האיש אשר יבחר יהוה הוא הקדוש" (במדבר טז, ז) - ולא \'הטהור\'. תרין דרגין אינון - קדוש וטהור. כהן - קדוש, לוי - טהור. ועל דא "הקדוש" כתיב.'
add(62,'zohar-priest-holy-levite-pure',12,'Zohar III, 176b',zoharHoly,
'Therefore, “The man whom Hashem chooses—he is the holy one,” and not “the pure one.” There are two levels: holy and pure. The priest is holy; the Levite is pure. That is why “the holy one” is written.',
'This is the precise Zohar distinction cited here. The surrounding discussion associates priesthood with the right side and lovingkindness, and the Levite with the side of judgment.',P62,url='https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/זהר_חלק_ג_קעו_ב')
E[-1]['sourceEvidence'][0]['webRef']='turn54view1'
add(62,'peri-tzadik-purity-and-holiness-of-the-heart',12,'Peri Tzadik, Ki Teitzei 7:1',sub(local('peri-tzadik','Peri Tzadik, Ki Teitzei 7:1',0,None),'וכמו שכ\' בזוה"ק (קרח שם)','ובמשה רבינו'),
'As the Zohar says in Korach: The Levite is pure; the priest is holy. For the Levites come from the left side, which is the aspect of purity: to purify the “fool’s heart at his left” and empty it of evil. The priest is holy: to bring holiness into the “wise person’s heart at his right,” for the priest comes from lovingkindness on the right and is holy. The Levite comes from the left side, fear; and it is written, “The fear of Hashem is pure.” He is called pure because, through fear, he purifies the fool’s heart.',
'The parallel explains the difference in terms of inner work: purification removes evil, while holiness brings divine service into the heart. It adds the meaning of the distinction to the Zohar’s terminology.',P62,url='#/read/Peri_Tzadik,_Ki_Teitzei_7',readerRef='Peri Tzadik, Ki Teitzei 7:1')
add(62,'dover-torah-is-not-an-automatic-inheritance',17,'Dover Tzedek, Miscellany 4:86',local('dover-tzedek','Dover Tzedek, Miscellany 4:86',14,23),
'From the aspect of the Oral Torah, a person bequeaths his power to his children, because the child derives from the father’s essence. Just as the offspring of Avraham, Yitzchak, and Yaakov are, from birth, from the womb, and from conception, offspring blessed by Hashem and holy offspring, so it should be with Torah: one who is the very essence of Torah, whose powers are all of this character, should be able to bequeath it to his children. Yet Moshe our teacher, peace be upon him, was the very essence of the Torah, which is called by his name, and he did not leave his crown to his children; rather, the crown of Torah lies available to everyone. For from the aspect of the Written Torah, which is Hashem’s Torah, he has no relationship with it that would make it exclusively his, so that he could bequeath it to his children. Only from the aspect of the Oral Torah, which is “his Torah,” would it be fitting for him to bequeath it; yet it is still not completely acquired, for “at first,” and so forth [it is called Hashem’s Torah]. Throughout a person’s life, the same beginning and end remain: at every level where he stands, he is at the end of what preceded it and at the beginning of what follows it. He always still needs labor and effort. With every teaching of Torah, he does not bless over the Torah at the beginning; only at the end does he recognize this, and it is then called “his Torah” retroactively.',
'The passage first raises the possibility of inheritance and then expressly limits it: Torah remains a continuing task of effort and acquisition. This explains why Moshe’s crown is open to all rather than secured for his biological descendants.',P62,url='#/read/Dover_Tzedek,_Miscellany_4',readerRef='Dover Tzedek, Miscellany 4:86')
add(62,'eruvin-both-are-divine-words',23,'Eruvin 13b:10-11',he('Eruvin 13b',10)+' '+he('Eruvin 13b',11),
'Rabbi Abba said in the name of Shmuel: For three years Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel disagreed. These said, “The law follows our view,” and those said, “The law follows our view.” A heavenly voice went forth and said: Both these and those are the words of the living God, and the law follows Beit Hillel. Since both these and those are the words of the living God, why did Beit Hillel merit having the law established according to their view? Because they were gentle and forbearing, and studied both their own words and the words of Beit Shammai. Moreover, they placed Beit Shammai’s words before their own.',
'The original passage preserves both the truth represented by the differing views and the practical decision in favor of Beit Hillel. Rav Tzadok explains how distinct spiritual qualities can give rise to those different views.',P62)
add(62,'resisei-different-qualities-and-disagreement',23,'Resisei Layla 48:2',local('resisei-layla','Resisei Layla 48:2',12,33),
'Concerning a disagreement for the sake of Heaven, our Sages said: “Both these and those are the words of the living God.” Both are called “the city of our God” and the counsel of Hashem, for the blessed God created different views. Yet from this also emerged the disagreement of Korach and his congregation. The Zohar says that Korach was the beginning of disagreement in the manner of anger. The meaning is as follows: As is known, “the world is built through lovingkindness.” The beginning of creation comes from the attribute of lovingkindness associated with Avraham. This is God’s kindness in drawing forth abundance so that a creature should exist; from this aspect, the flow extends to every creature with the greatest possible perfection. If so, there could be no division into different creatures, for such division necessarily involves one lacking a quality that another has. Therefore, on the second day, the attribute of restriction was created: the left hand that pushes away, opposite the right hand that draws near. This produced distinctions: this creature’s attainment reaches this far, and that creature’s this far. From this came the attainment of Yitzchak our father, who followed Avraham, the first to attain knowledge that there is a Creator of the world. He was not satisfied with his father’s attainment, but sought an additional, distinct attainment. As is known, that is why “God of” is said separately concerning each of the forefathers: each attained something particular and distinct. Yitzchak introduced this recognition: there are different attainments and different paths to reaching God’s truth. Otherwise, one creature and one day of creation would have sufficed. Since God distinguished and formed different creatures, there must be different and distinct ways of praising Him, each true in itself and in its own place. This is the quality of the forefathers, among whom there is no actual disagreement: Avraham’s and Yitzchak’s qualities—love and fear—are both true together, and one must serve God through both. Each is true in itself and according to its level, like the upper and lower waters and the firmament separating them, each fully existing in its own place. But in the children afterward—that is, as these qualities spread—each already contained both qualities, though one quality predominated in one and the other in another. From this came disagreement: concerning the very same matter, one says this and another says that. For they are all also joined and united through their mutual inclusion; therefore they judge the same matter differently.',
'Rav Tzadok connects differing judgments to differing proportions of shared spiritual qualities. The qualification “for the sake of Heaven” belongs to the source’s opening statement and is retained here.',P62,url='#/read/Resisei_Layla_48',readerRef='Resisei Layla 48:2')
add(63,'peri-tzadik-wise-son-and-mothers-grief',2,'Peri Tzadik, Kedoshim 3:1',sub(local('peri-tzadik','Peri Tzadik, Kedoshim 3:1',0,None),'אך הענין עפמ"ש','וז"ש אמו'),
'The matter is explained by what is said in Eduyot: A father confers upon his son, among other things, wisdom; the influence of wisdom comes to the son from the father. The mother, who is not obligated in Torah study and is not obligated to teach her son Torah, brings fear of Hashem into the son, for she is called “a woman who fears Hashem.” This is what Scripture says: “A wise son gladdens his father, but a foolish son is his mother’s grief.” The son’s wisdom comes principally from the father, and the father rejoices. A foolish son means one who lacks fear of Heaven, as the Sages expounded: “Why is there a price in the hand of a fool to acquire wisdom, when he has no heart?”—those who engage in Torah but have no fear of Heaven. Because he lacks fear of Heaven, even though he studies Torah, he is nevertheless called a fool; for the essence of Torah is that it shows a person the way of Hashem. Since he lacks fear of Heaven, it is “a price in the hand of a fool.” Therefore it is written, “A foolish son”—who has no fear of Heaven—“is his mother’s grief,” because she did not instill fear of Heaven in him.',
'This parallel explains the specific reference to the mother’s grief through her role in instilling reverence. Later in the same passage Rav Tzadok also recognizes the mother’s share in the child’s Torah learning through helping him attend to it.',P63,url='#/read/Peri_Tzadik,_Kedoshim_3',readerRef='Peri Tzadik, Kedoshim 3:1')
E[-1]['sourceEvidence'][0]['contextHe']=sub(local('peri-tzadik','Peri Tzadik, Kedoshim 3:1',0,None),'ומ"מ גם')
add(63,'bereshit-rabbah-daughter-at-mothers-home',5,'Bereshit Rabbah 60:7',sub(he('Bereshit Rabbah 60:7'),end='(בראשית כד, כט)'),
'“The young woman ran and told her mother’s household.” Rabbi Yochanan said: A woman ordinarily frequents her mother’s household. They objected to him: But is it not written, “She told her father”? He said to them: Her mother had died; whom else should she have told, if not her father?',
'The Midrash supplies the background for the ois’s phrase “the daughter’s habitual presence with her mother.” It speaks about ordinary familiarity and includes the case in which the mother had died.',P63)
add(63,'takanat-sons-reveal-hidden-depths-of-fathers',11,'Takanat HaShavin 6:65',local('takanat-hashavin','Takanat HaShavin 6:65',0,8),
'But it is known that the children clarify the fathers. As I heard, the depths of the fathers’ hearts and what is hidden within them are revealed in the children. It is also known that every clarification must come through its opposite. Therefore Avraham fathered Yitzchak: his desire was to clarify that Avraham’s lovingkindness was only for the sake of Heaven. The dross of Yishmael, who came from him, was not called his offspring; this was clarified when he fathered Yitzchak, who is called his offspring and in whom the depths of his heart are revealed. Yitzchak’s quality, on the contrary, was restriction and fear for the sake of Heaven. This clarified that the lovingkindness manifest in Avraham our father was also only for the sake of Heaven. He was not like the nations of the world, who are immersed in one quality and cannot change it; in his depths he also possessed the opposite quality, and if it were needed for the sake of Heaven, he would use it as well, as happened at the binding of Yitzchak.',
'This gives a developed instance of the teaching Rav Tzadok says he received: Yitzchak’s opposite quality reveals that Avraham’s kindness was a free devotion to God rather than an unchangeable temperament.',P63,url='#/read/Takanat_HaShavin_6',readerRef='Takanat HaShavin 6:65')
add(64,'tosafot-harosh-voice-in-honor-of-rabbi-eliezer',10,'Tosafot HaRosh on Berakhot 52a:1',he('Tosafot HaRosh on Berakhot 52a',1),
'“It follows Rabbi Yehoshua, who says that we do not heed a heavenly voice.” This is difficult: Rabbi Yehoshua said this concerning the disagreement between Rabbi Eliezer and the Sages, and we too do not follow Rabbi Eliezer or the heavenly voice there. But here, concerning Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel, we follow the heavenly voice; perhaps Rabbi Yehoshua agrees in this case as well. The answer is that Rabbi Yehoshua’s formulation—“We do not heed a heavenly voice,” because “It is not in heaven”—implies that a heavenly voice should be disregarded in every case. We, however, follow the heavenly voice in favor of Beit Hillel because Beit Hillel were the majority; a heavenly voice was needed because Beit Shammai were sharper. But the heavenly voice for Rabbi Eliezer came only in Rabbi Eliezer’s honor, and [following it] would contradict the verse “Incline after the majority.”',
'Tosafot HaRosh distinguishes a heavenly voice that resolves a doubt while supporting the majority from the voice honoring Rabbi Eliezer against the majority. This provides a direct original-source explanation for the lack of practical authority of the latter voice.',P64)
add(64,'rav-nissim-heavenly-voice-as-a-test',11,'Rav Nissim Gaon on Berakhot 19b:1',sub(he('Rav Nissim Gaon on Berakhot 19b',1),end='וכל מקום בתלמוד'),
'Concerning the statement that a heavenly voice went forth and said, “What have you against Rabbi Eliezer, whose view the law follows everywhere?” I have seen two answers. The first is that the heavenly voice did not say that the law followed him in this matter; it said “everywhere” without specification. It is possible that it meant everywhere except this case, or similar cases in which the law does not follow Rabbi Eliezer. Thus the words of the heavenly voice would remain correct and valid. The second is that its purpose was only to test the Sages: would they abandon the tradition in their possession and the teaching in their mouths because of a heavenly voice, or not? This resembles what is written, “For Hashem your God is testing you.” Through all this, the soundness of their tradition became known. This is what Rabbi Yehoshua said: “It is not in heaven.” That is, Hashem’s Torah is perfect and has already been given to us at Sinai; He made known that He does not replace a single word of it. Our Torah contains no deficiency or uncertainty that would require proof from heaven.',
'Rav Nissim presents testing the Sages as one of two explanations, both retained here. Rav Tzadok develops his own account of the divine assistance given to Rabbi Eliezer; the shared point is that a heavenly sign does not displace the Torah’s received rules of judgment.',P64)
add(64,'ibn-ezra-you-caused-their-deaths',12,'Ibn Ezra on Numbers 17:6:2',he('Ibn Ezra on Numbers 17:6',2),
'“You have killed”—the meaning is: What proof did this provide that the tribe of Levi was chosen and that Aharon was chosen as High Priest? Perhaps through your prayer, or through wisdom that you possessed, you burned those who brought the offering.',
'Ibn Ezra explains the complaint as a challenge to what the miracle proved: the people imagined that Moshe and Aharon’s own spiritual power had brought about the deaths. This is the complaint Rav Tzadok develops.',P64)
add(64,'kedushat-levi-divine-decree-and-prayer',15,'Kedushat Levi, Numbers, Korach 7',he('Kedushat Levi, Numbers, Korach',7),
'“For it is not from my own heart.” This also explains the verse “You have killed the people of Hashem.” The principle is that sometimes a righteous person brings about something even though it was not decreed above; nevertheless, through his prayer, the righteous person accomplishes it. Sometimes the righteous person draws down an action from above that was decreed above. Here Israel suspected that Moshe our teacher and Aharon, through their righteousness, had accomplished this through their prayer. That is what they meant by “You have killed the people of Hashem.” Therefore Moshe our teacher said, “For it is not from my own heart”: this matter was decreed by the Holy One, blessed be He; we did not bring it about through our prayer.',
'Kedushat Levi distinguishes the people’s suspicion from Moshe’s answer. The note retains both, so that the explanation of their complaint is not mistaken for the source’s endorsement of it.',P64)
add(65,'radak-the-way-of-all-the-earth',1,'Radak on Psalms 139:24:1-2',he('Radak on Psalms 139:24',1)+' '+he('Radak on Psalms 139:24',2),
'“A grievous way”—a way of rebellion, as in “They rebelled and grieved His holy spirit.” “And lead me”—if You see any rebellious way in me, put me to death immediately. This is “the way of the world,” as in “I am going the way of all the earth,” and “A person goes to his everlasting home.”',
'Radak reads “the way of the world” here as death, the way of all the earth. This is the reading of the verse needed to understand Rav Tzadok’s opening statement.',P65)
add(65,'zohar-offering-the-soul-in-prayer',3,'Zohar II, 202b',
'למסור נפשיה לגביה, ולמיהב ליה נשמתיה ברעותא דלבא, כד נפלין על אנפין ואמרין "אליך יהו"ה נפשי אשא" (תהלים כה א), דיכוין לביה ורעותיה לגביה לממסר ליה נפשיה ברעותא שלים.',
'To surrender one’s soul to Him and give Him one’s soul with the heart’s willing intention, when falling upon one’s face and saying, “To You, Hashem, I lift my soul”: one should direct one’s heart and will toward Him, to surrender one’s soul to Him with complete willingness.',
'The Zohar lists this as the sixth of the commandments expressed in prayer. It describes the intention of surrender during nefilat apayim, directly explaining the place of that practice after prayer.',P65,url='https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/זהר_חלק_ב_רב_ב')
E[-1]['sourceEvidence'][0]['webRef']='turn54view2'
add(65,'bamidbar-rabbah-korachs-incomplete-vision',8,'Bamidbar Rabbah 18:8',sub(he('Bamidbar Rabbah 18:8'),'וקרח שפקח'),
'But Korach, who was astute, what led him to this foolishness? His eye misled him. He saw a great lineage arising from him: Shmuel, who is equal to Moshe and Aharon, as it is stated, “Moshe and Aharon among His priests, and Shmuel among those who call His name”; and twenty-four divisions arising from his descendants, all prophesying with the holy spirit, as it is stated, “All these were sons of Heman.” He said: Could such greatness be destined to arise from me, and I remain silent? But he did not see correctly: his sons repented, and these descendants arose from them. Moshe, however, did see. That is why Korach joined in, relying on the assurance he had heard from Moshe’s mouth that all would perish and one would survive: “The man whom Hashem chooses—he is the holy one.”',
'The Midrash explains both Korach’s vision and the error in his inference. His descendants’ greatness came through his sons’ repentance; it did not guarantee that Korach himself would be chosen.',P65)
add(65,'bamidbar-rabbah-the-leaders-in-korachs-company',10,'Bamidbar Rabbah 18:3',sub(he('Bamidbar Rabbah 18:3'),'וכשאמר משה'),
'When Moshe said, “They shall put a thread of tekhelet on the corner fringe,” Korach immediately ordered two hundred and fifty garments of tekhelet to be made. The two hundred and fifty heads of Sanhedrin who rose against Moshe wrapped themselves in them, as it is stated, “They rose before Moshe, two hundred and fifty men from the children of Israel, leaders of the congregation, those summoned to assembly.” Korach arose and made them a feast, and they wrapped themselves in garments of tekhelet. Aharon’s sons came to take their priestly gifts, the breast and the thigh. They opposed them and said: Who commanded you to take this? Was it not Moshe? We will give nothing; God did not say this. They came and told Moshe. He went to appease them, and they immediately rose against him to confront him before Moshe. “Two hundred and fifty men from the children of Israel”—who were they? Elitzur son of Shedeur and his companions, “these men who were designated by name.” Although Scripture did not identify them openly, it gave their distinguishing signs, and from the verses you can recognize them. To what may this be compared? To a well-born man found stealing objects from a bathhouse. The owner did not want to expose him, so he began giving his distinguishing signs. When asked who had stolen his objects, he said: That well-born man, tall, with fine teeth, black hair, and a handsome nose. Once he gave the signs, they knew who he was. Here too, although Scripture left their identities unstated, it gave their signs, and you know who they were. Elsewhere it says, “These were those summoned from the congregation, the leaders of their ancestral tribes, heads of Israel’s thousands,” and “Moshe and Aharon took these men who were designated by name.” Here it says, “Leaders of the congregation, those summoned to assembly, men of renown,” and “They assembled against Moshe and Aharon.”',
'This passage identifies the company both as heads of Sanhedrin and as Elitzur and his fellow tribal leaders. Its comparison of the two biblical descriptions supplies the basis for Rav Tzadok’s reference to the twelve princes.',P65)
add(65,'taanit-the-nazirs-affliction-of-his-soul',13,'Taanit 11a:14-17',' '.join(he('Taanit 11a',i) for i in range(14,18)),
'Shmuel said: Anyone who observes a fast is called a sinner. He follows this tanna, as it was taught: Rabbi Elazar HaKappar the Great says: What does “He shall atone for him for having sinned against the soul” teach? Against what soul did this person sin? Rather, he afflicted himself by abstaining from wine. Is this not an argument from the lesser to the greater? If one who afflicted himself only by abstaining from wine is called a sinner, all the more so one who afflicts himself by abstaining from every kind of enjoyment. Rabbi Elazar says: He is called holy, as it is stated, “He shall be holy; he shall let the locks of his head grow.” If one who afflicted himself in only one respect is called holy, all the more so one who afflicts himself in every respect. But according to Shmuel, is he not called holy? That refers to the growth of his hair. And according to Rabbi Elazar, is he not called a sinner? That concerns one who rendered himself impure.',
'Rav Tzadok draws on the interpretation of sinning “against the soul” as self-affliction. The original exchange also records the opposing description of the nazir as holy and explains how each view reads the other verse.',P65)
add(65,'peri-tzadik-sinai-and-the-future-level',16,'Peri Tzadik, Korach 1:2',sub(local('peri-tzadik','Peri Tzadik, Korach 1:2',0,None),end='וזה ענין שנכתב'),
'This was Korach’s error: at the giving of the Torah, it was indeed fitting that the true rectification should come about as it will in the future. This was his claim: “The entire congregation is holy”—they all heard at Sinai, “I am Hashem your God”—“and Hashem is among them.” That is, as it will be in the future, when the Holy One, blessed be He, sits among them and they surround Him in a circle. In a circle there are no differences of level; thus, “Why do you exalt yourselves above the congregation of Hashem?” This is indeed so when the attribute of Atika is revealed, as at the giving of the Torah, when “Hashem spoke with you face to face.” Likewise, the revelation at the splitting of the sea came from Atika, as the Zohar says, “It depends on Atika.” The Mekhilta and the Zohar say that a maidservant at the sea saw what Yechezkel did not see: she saw as did the greatest person in Israel. All were equal, for when the revelation depends on Atika, there are no differences of level. So too at the giving of the Torah: “Israel encamped there,” which Rashi explains as one person with one heart; there were no differences of level, as it is stated, “Hashem spoke with you face to face.” But afterward this level was lost, and this revelation will not come until the future, when the true rectification arrives.',
'This parallel explains the counterfactual in the ois: Sinai opened the possibility of the future level, but that level was subsequently lost. Korach’s error was to regard the complete future state as already continuously present.',P65,url='#/read/Peri_Tzadik,_Korach_1',readerRef='Peri Tzadik, Korach 1:2')
add(65,'peri-tzadik-korachs-company-and-surrender',17,'Peri Tzadik, Korach 2:1',sub(local('peri-tzadik','Peri Tzadik, Korach 2:1',0,None),'ועל זה מרמז המדרש רבה'),
'This is what the Midrash Rabbah intimates: “Were they fools? Rather, they sinned against their own souls.” They thought and accepted upon themselves, in surrender of their souls, that if the incense should make clear that despite all this their souls were still not completely purified, then what need had they for life in this world? “And Korach, who was astute”—he was a great scholar in the wisdom of Torah and should have known that “there is no righteous person on earth” and that there are higher levels above higher levels in the chambers of Torah. “But his eye misled him,” because he possessed the holy spirit and saw a great lineage, and so it seemed to him that he would be the chosen one. This was clarified by “They descended alive to the underworld”: even in the underworld they remain in the fullness of their life in holiness. This resembles what was said of Adam: “Lest he stretch out his hand and take also from the Tree of Life, and eat and live forever.” As the Midrash Rabbah says, they remain alive and in existence until now; at that very moment it became clear to them that Moshe is true and his Torah is true. In the future they too are destined to ascend, as it is stated, “Hashem puts to death and brings to life; He brings down to the underworld and raises up.”',
'Rav Tzadok’s parallel supplies the interpretation of their willingness to risk their lives, while retaining the error in their self-assessment and the distinction between Korach’s vision and its fulfillment.',P65,url='#/read/Peri_Tzadik,_Korach_2',readerRef='Peri Tzadik, Korach 2:1')
add(65,'ramban-why-separate-from-the-congregation',22,'Ramban on Numbers 17:10:1',he('Ramban on Numbers 17:10',1),
'“Remove yourselves from this congregation”—I do not understand the reason for this verse and its counterpart above, “Separate yourselves from this congregation.” God has the power to kill many by plague around one righteous person, leaving that person alone alive, as occurred among the firstborn of Egypt. So too in every plague: three people sleep beneath one blanket, two die, and the one in the middle escapes. It appears that the wrath was going forth to kill the entire congregation as these sinners were killed, because they had followed them—through the opening of the earth’s mouth or the fire that came forth. These are encompassing blows, capable of killing everyone standing there together, unless a second miracle is performed for those who escape. Alternatively, the Holy One, blessed be He, said this in honor of the righteous: as long as they remained among them, He would not strike them. The purpose of these and similar statements is to make known that they need a plea for mercy and atonement, and Moshe is the one who hastens to do this immediately.',
'This is Ramban’s question, followed by his own two explanations and their practical purpose. Rav Tzadok takes up the question and offers the further explanation developed in this ois.',P65)

